virtiofs: Add uid= and gid= mount options that set every file's owner

Made against Linux 6.18.57 (Guest/kernel/VERSION). Guest/kernel/build-kernel.sh applies it
with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` and fails the build if it does not apply cleanly.

maw mounts the owner's macOS home directory at /mnt/mac through virtiofs. Apple's virtiofs
server reports as the owner of a file the uid and gid of the guest process whose request
fetched its attributes, and the guest caches that answer: a file that root looked up first
shows as root's to every user until the cache expires. Programs that check ownership, such as
git, then refuse a directory of the default user. docs/decisions/0021-mac-share.md records the
measurements.

With uid=<uid> and gid=<gid>, every inode of the mount shows that uid and gid, whoever looks
first, as WSL's drvfs shows its uid= and gid= for /mnt/c. The values are checked as any mount's
uid= and gid= are (fsparam_uid, fsparam_gid). virtiofs already mounts with default_permissions,
so the guest checks each access against these owners and the file's mode: the named user is the
owner, root is root, and other users get the group and other bits. The server still performs
every access with the macOS user's rights. Without the options, nothing changes.

Only virtiofs accepts the options; the other FUSE file systems never set the fields.

Tests: MacShareIntegrationTests in Tests/mawdTests, through scripts/integration-test.sh.

